NET current expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":87946,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":2647000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":29139,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":32864,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2615.22,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":202.54,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"South Hams faces \u00a3394,000 budget gap for 2027/28 despite identifying \u00a32m in savings","lead":"South Hams District Council identified \u00a32.071m in savings and cost reductions for 2027/28 but still faces a shortfall of \u00a3394,542, according to its medium-term financial strategy approved at the executive meeting on 16 September. The gap represents 2.6 per cent of the council's projected net budget of \u00a315.152m. Larger deficits are forecast for the following two years: \u00a31.455m in 2028/29 and \u00a31.165m in 2029/30. The council's earmarked reserves are being depleted rapidly to fund capital projects and to cover two major contractor failures \u2014 a waste service collapse that cost \u00a34m to bring back in-house, and the administration of leisure contractor Fusion, which required \u00a31.2m to restore services. The council's reserves are projected to fall from \u00a313.957m to \u00a36.731m by March 2028. Government funding cuts have compounded the pressure: the Revenue Support Grant falls from \u00a33.7m to \u00a31.8m over three years, and a business rates reset eliminated \u00a32.5m of income.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Medium Term Financial Strategy 2027/28 to 2029/30","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36566/36566.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"South Hams faces \u00a37m reserve depletion as government funding falls","lead":"South Hams faces sharp depletion of its earmarked reserves [money set aside for named purposes], projected to fall from \u00a313.957m to \u00a36.731m by March 2028, driven by falls in central government funding and business rates income. The council's government grant is falling from \u00a33.7m to \u00a31.8m over three years, while business rates income fell sharply from \u00a34.5m to \u00a32m. Rural councils face spending power growth of only 1% to 2029, compared with 21% for urban authorities, according to the rural services network. Despite collecting 98.51% of council tax (8th nationally) and 98.68% of business rates (top quartile), South Hams faces an unresolved budget gap of \u00a3394,542 for 2027/28 with larger shortfalls forecast ahead.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Medium Term Financial Strategy 2027/28 to 2029/30","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36566/36566.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"South Hams faces budget gaps of nearly \u00a34m over three years as reserves drain from contractor failures and funding cuts","lead":"South Hams District Council identified cost pressures of \u00a32.347m and savings of \u00a32.071m at its executive meeting on 16 September, but still faces a budget gap of \u00a3395,000 for 2027/28 and larger shortfalls forecast for the two years after. The council's earmarked reserves, money set aside for named purposes, are projected to fall from \u00a313.957m to \u00a36.731m by March 2028 \u2014 a decline from 115 per cent to 60 per cent of its annual net budget \u2014 mainly because of two major contractor failures. The waste service contract collapsed, costing \u00a34m to bring the service back in-house and restore collections. Leisure operator Fusion went into administration in April 2026, requiring \u00a31.2m from reserves to maintain continuity until a new provider took over in June. The council faces a steeper structural problem: government funding cuts have reduced its Revenue Support Grant from \u00a33.7m to a projected \u00a31.8m by 2028/29, and a business rates baseline reset eliminated \u00a32.5m of annual income. The council's collection rates for council tax and business rates remain strong, at 98.51 and 98.68 per cent respectively, but the Senior Leadership Team must now find further savings to close the remaining gaps.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Medium Term Financial Strategy 2027/28 to 2029/30","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36566/36566.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"South Hams Executive","paper_title":"Appendix C \u2013 Transfers to/from Reserves","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36569/36569.pdf","headline":"Local Plan team and Planning Policy salary costs are being funded from reserves rather than operating budgets, with Local Plan withdrawals jumping to \u00a3290,000 in 2026/27.","detail":"The council is withdrawing from reserves to cover salary costs: Planning Policy and Major Developments drew \u00a398,000 in 2025/26 for salaries; the Local Plan team salary funding increased from \u00a380,000 in 2025/26 to \u00a3290,000 in 2026/27. This pattern suggests operating budgets may not be fully covering these service salaries and the council is relying on reserve drawdown.","category":"overspend","matters":"Funding operational salary costs from reserves rather than recurrent budgets is a sign of structural budget pressure and raises questions about the council's underlying budget sustainability.","figures":"Planning Policy salaries: \u00a398,000 (2025/26); Local Plan salaries: \u00a380,000 (2025/26) and \u00a3290,000 (2026/27)","quote":"Local Plan (to part fund salaries of the Local Plan team)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"South Hams Executive","paper_title":"Appendix C \u2013 Transfers to/from Reserves","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36569/36569.pdf","headline":"Reserve withdrawals from Business Rates Retention Reserve increase to \u00a3500,000 in 2027/28, up from \u00a3300,000 in earlier years.","detail":"The council is drawing down the Business Rates Retention Reserve more heavily in the outer year. Total withdrawals across all reserves also rise from \u00a3380,000 in 2026/27 to \u00a3790,000 in 2027/28\u2014more than double. This increasing reliance on reserves occurs at the same time that NHB funding is being lost.","category":"reserves","matters":"A lender would need to understand whether these rising drawdowns reflect planned depletion of reserves to fund specific items, or whether they signal structural budget pressure that reserves are being used to bridge.","figures":"\u00a3500,000 (2027/28 BRRR withdrawal); total reserves drawn: \u00a3380,000 (2026/27) increasing to \u00a3790,000 (2027/28)","quote":"Business Rates Retention Reserve (300,000) (500,000)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"South Hams Executive","paper_title":"Appendix C \u2013 Transfers to/from Reserves","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36569/36569.pdf","headline":"New Homes Bonus scheme ends after 2025/26, eliminating a funding stream and reducing total reserve contributions by \u00a3686,300 in 2027/28.","detail":"The council receives New Homes Bonus funding of \u00a3530,422 in 2025/26, but the scheme terminates after that year. Total contributions to earmarked reserves decline from \u00a31,248,300 in 2026/27 to \u00a3562,000 in 2027/28\u2014a reduction of \u00a3686,300. This swing reflects both the loss of NHB and the council's adjustment to a smaller future reserve position.","category":"income","matters":"A lender assessing the council's financial sustainability needs to understand how it will absorb the loss of \u00a3530k+ in recurring funding and whether operating budgets are configured to function without it.","figures":"\u00a3530,422 (2025/26); reduction of \u00a3686,300 between 2026/27 (\u00a31,248,300) and 2027/28 (\u00a3562,000)","quote":"(2025/26 is the last year of the NHB scheme)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"South Hams Executive","paper_title":"Appendix A: Integrated Performance Management Report: April \u2013 July 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36550/36550.pdf","headline":"Business Continuity & Capacity flagged as strategic risk at level 9","detail":"Business Continuity & Capacity is identified as a strategic risk scored at level 9. Specific risks and mitigations are not detailed in this report extract.","category":"governance","matters":"Strategic risks to operational capacity and business continuity could impair the council's ability to deliver services and maintain financial controls","figures":"Strategic Risk Score: 9","quote":"SRR4 \u2013 Business Continuity & Capacity","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"South Hams Executive","paper_title":"Appendix A: Integrated Performance Management Report: April \u2013 July 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36550/36550.pdf","headline":"Medium Term Financial Strategy flagged as strategic risk at level 9","detail":"The report identifies the Medium Term Financial Strategy as a strategic risk scored at level 9 (high). The specific risks and mitigation are noted as being detailed on following pages but are not included in this report.","category":"governance","matters":"Strategic-level risks to financial strategy directly affect financial sustainability, borrowing capacity, and medium-term financial planning credibility","figures":"Strategic Risk Score: 9","quote":"SRR1 \u2013 Medium Term Financial Strategy","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"South Hams","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"Local Plan team and Planning Policy salary costs are being funded from reserves rather than operating budgets, with Local Plan withdrawals jumping to \u00a3290,000 in 2026/27.","lead":"Funding operational salary costs from reserves rather than recurrent budgets is a sign of structural budget pressure and raises questions about the council's underlying budget sustainability.","also":["27 other findings in this window"],"watch":null,"n_findings":28,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix C \u2013 Transfers to/from Reserves","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.swdevon.gov.uk/documents/s36569/36569.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":131,"news_drafts_total":3}}